{"answers":[{"question_id":"Q003_actin_like_atpase_divergence","answer":"SCOPe 2.08 places these catalytic domains in separate families within the same Actin-like ATPase domain superfamily, c.55.1 (SUNID 53067): Actin/HSP70 is c.55.1.1, hexokinase is c.55.1.3, and glycerol kinase is c.55.1.4. This superfamily belongs to the Ribonuclease H-like motif fold, c.55, in the alpha/beta class, c.

Their shared superfamily supports probable common evolutionary ancestry despite substantial sequence and functional divergence, with an inherited structural framework for nucleotide-dependent chemistry. This is stronger evolutionary evidence than sharing only a fold or class, which alone does not establish homology. Family membership indicates closer relationships, but even Actin/HSP70 includes distinct cellular functions.

The relationship does not justify transferring actin filament formation, HSP70 chaperone activity, hexokinase sugar specificity, or glycerol kinase glycerol specificity among them. ATP hydrolysis and ATP-dependent substrate phosphorylation are also distinct activities. A related domain can guide comparison of the conserved structural core, but precise function, catalytic residues, substrate recognition, and template suitability require sequence/structure alignment, active-site and ligand evidence, and biochemical validation. The classification concerns the relevant domains, not necessarily every domain or the complete proteins.","evidence_used":["Pretrained model knowledge","mcp__scope__search_scop","mcp__scope__get_scop_node","mcp__scope__list_scop_children","/api/v1/releases/current","/api/v1/releases/2.08/search?q=hexokinase&mode=auto&limit=10","/api/v1/releases/2.08/search?q=glycerol+kinase&mode=auto&limit=10","/api/v1/releases/2.08/sccs/c.55","/api/v1/releases/2.08/sccs/c.55/parents","/api/v1/releases/2.08/sccs/c.55.1","/api/v1/releases/2.08/sccs/c.55.1/parents","/api/v1/releases/2.08/sccs/c.55.1/children?limit=20"]}]}
